  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA warm front continues to approach the region, which has\nresulted in increasing clouds. This will also result in rain \nshowers moving into the area from west to east by later this\nafternoon and this evening\n\nThe most concentrated period of rain showers will be tonight/early\nSunday morning. PWATs will be around 1.7 inches and coupled \nwith a decent LLJ, locally heavy rainfall will be possible. The \ngreatest threat for this will be across Northeast PA-Catskills.\nLatest trends have been for the axis of heavier rainfall to be\nlocated slightly farther west, perhaps right through the\nScranton area. Since this is a heavily urbanized area, this is\ncurrently where the greatest threat for localized flash flooding\nis. That being said, with drier antecedent conditions and the\nlocalized nature of the flash flooding threat, the decision was\nmade after coordinating with surrounding WFOs to hold off on\nissuing a Flood Watch. A shorter-fused Flood Watch may be issued\nif the threat for Flash Flooding increases.\n\nThere will likely be a bit of a lull in the rainfall Sunday\nmorning, before a cold front will bring another round of\nscattered showers and thunderstorms in the afternoon. There\nshould be some destabilization in the afternoon, but lapse rates\ncontinue to look poor at this time. This will likely limit the\nsevere thunderstorm potential for Sunday afternoon/early\nevening, but still cannot rule out an isolated severe\nthunderstorm.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFollowing the passage of the cold front, cooler air will move\ninto the region, which will bring highs in the 60s to lower 70s\nboth Monday and Tuesday. High pressure will keep conditions dry\nduring this period. Another system may bring some scattered\nshowers and potentially some thunderstorms by the middle of\nnext week, along with warmer temperatures.",
